{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>And you shall make a breastplate of judgment, the work of a weaver.</b> Thus was the making of the breastplate: woven as the work of a weaver from tekhelet, from argaman, from tola'at shani, and from linen. He would take one thread of gold and set it upon six threads of tekhelet, and fold the seven threads together as one; and likewise he would do with one thread of gold together with six of argaman, and one thread of gold together with six of linen. This is what is written: תכלת וארגמן ותולעת שני ושש — twenty-eight threads, as it is written (Exodus 39): ״וירקעו את פחי הזהב וקצץ פתילם לעשות בתוך התכלת ובתוך הארגמן ובתוך תולעת השני ובתוך השש״ — this teaches that a thread of gold was folded within them. The length of the breastplate was two spans and its width one span, folded in two, so it was a span upon a span, square. Set in it were four rows of stone, and every stone was square, and engraved upon them were the names of the twelve tribes upon twelve stones. Reuven was engraved upon the stone odem, Shim'on upon pitdah, and so all of them. Besides the twelve tribes that were written upon these twelve stones, the names of the patriarchs were also written — Avraham, Yitzhak, Ya'akov — and שבטי ישרון. And in this order they were written: Reuven — א — upon odem; Shim'on — ב — upon pitdah; Levi — רה״ם — upon bareket; Yehudah — י — upon nophekh; Yissakhar — צ — upon sapir; Zevulun — ח — upon yahalom; Dan — ק יע״ק — upon leshem; Naftali — ב — upon shvo; Gad — שבט״י — upon ahlamah; Asher — יש״ר — upon tarshish; Yosef — ו״ן — upon shoham; Binyamin — upon yashfeh. So you find upon every stone six letters, to indicate that the six days of creation depend upon the twelve tribes. And there were seventy-two letters in the stones of the breastplate, for twelve times six is seventy-two, corresponding to the seventy-two letters of the Great Name, to indicate thereby the endurance of the world, which was created in seventy-two hours — for the world was created 'on the day,' as it says (Genesis 2:4): ״ביום עשות YHWH Elohim ארץ ושמים״ — and the six days of creation are seventy-two hours, there being twelve hours in each day. This is what Scripture says (Psalms 89:3): ״אמרתי עולם חסד יבנה״ — as the numerical value of חס״ד, and we learn from this that the world, which was created in seventy-two hours, endures by virtue of the tribes, in whom there are seventy-two letters." "<b>And here,</b> Binyamin, who was engraved upon yashfeh, was complete and had no supporting letters from the patriarchs as did the rest of the tribes — for there survived of the tribe of Binyamin from the incident of the concubine at Give'ah only six hundred men — by virtue of Binyamin, who was engraved on the stones of the breastplate with six letters." "<b>And you should know</b> that it is found written in books of the wisdom of nature that all the precious stones that are foundational and root-originating are but twelve, and they are the ancestors of all other stones, all the rest being their branches and their offspring. <br>And the reason an odem stone was given to <b>Reuven</b> is because his face grew red in the sin with Bilhah when he disturbed his father's bed, and he confessed and was not ashamed. This stone is called rubin, and it grows in certain places in the sea; it is a large rock, and its source is like the source of silver and like the source of gold. That rock and its source they call balash\"u, and the rubin and the balash\"u are all one kind and one piece, except that the rubin is red in the extreme — and so is its Aramaic translation, סמקן — and it is the choicest of the stone, with branch-like protrusions that jut out from it and extend from it, while the balash\"u is far beneath it in the quality of redness. Because of this, the choicest of this stone is called in our language odem, for its appearance is red as blood, and its special property is that any woman who wears it never miscarries, and they say it is beneficial for a woman who has difficulty in childbirth; and if one grinds it and mixes it into food and drink, it is very beneficial for conception, like the duda'im that Reuven found, in which there was the likeness of a human form (דמות אדם). Therefore אדם is written deficient of the letter vav, to allude to אדם, for the written text alludes to the essence of the stone, while the deficient spelling without the vav alludes to its action. For this reason Reuven was engraved upon the odem stone. <br><b>Shim'on</b> was engraved upon pitdah; it is a green stone like keration of the sea, and so its Aramaic translation is ירקן; it is called prashma, and it is a kind of nophekh, which is markadi, which is also green like keration. And know that prashma and markadi are all one kind and one piece, as I explained to you regarding rubin and balash\"u, except that the markadi is the choicest, the select and cut from it, and it is green in the extreme and its luster gleams and its light flashes exceedingly, while the prashma is green also but far beneath it both in greenness and in the luster of light. And the reason this stone was given to Shim'on is because the face of Zimri son of Salu, prince of the tribe of Shim'on, grew green, as did the face of all those of the tribe of Shim'on who sinned with the daughters of Moav — and likewise our Rabbis, of blessed memory, darshened: a sign of transgression is jaundice (הדרוקן). The special property of this pitdah stone is that it cools the body, and therefore it is found in the lands of Kush, which are hot and steeped in licentiousness and need it to cool them, and this is what is written (Job 28:19): ״לא יערכנה פטדת כוש״ — Scripture attributes it to Kush, for it is found in the land of Kush. <br><b>Levi</b> was engraved upon bareket; it is the carbuncle, called bareket because it sparkles like lightning (ברק) and shines like a lamp, and so its Aramaic translation is ברקן. It is the stone that Noah suspended in the ark, from what is written (Genesis 6:16): ״צהר תעשה לתיבה״, and the prophet called it ekdach, as it says (Isaiah 54:12): ״ושעריך לאבני אקדח״, because it burns like a coal, from the language of (Isaiah 50:11) ״קודחי אש״. It was given to Levi because the tribe of Levi shone in Torah, as it says (Deuteronomy 33:10): ״יורו משפטיך ליעקב ותורתך לישראל״, and likewise Moshe, who was of the tribe of Levi — when he was born, the entire house was filled with light, and it is said of him that ״קרן עור פניו״, and it is written (Ecclesiastes 8:1): ״חכמת אדם תאיר פניו״. The special property of this stone is that it makes the simple wise and illuminates the eyes, besides that one uses the stone itself as one uses the light of a lamp; and they say that if one grinds it and mixes it into food and drink or with other medicaments, it is very beneficial for making one wise and for opening the heart. <br><b>Yehudah</b> was engraved upon nophekh; it is markadi, and in Arabic it is zumora, and the Aramaic translation combines both these terms — and therefore it says אזמרגדין, compounded from the term zumora and the term markadi. The stone is green like keration and is of the prashma kind as I mentioned. It was given to Yehudah because his face grew green in the incident of Tamar, and he conquered his inclination and confessed and was not ashamed; and his face grew green also because his father suspected him concerning Yosef, as it is written (Genesis 37:20): ״חיה רעה אכלתהו״, and when he came to bless his sons he said to him (Genesis 49:9): ״מטרף בני עלית״ — then his face became bright and gleaming like the nophekh, whose luster shines, as it is written of Yehudah (Genesis 49:8): ״ידך בערף אויביך״. The special property of this stone is that whoever carries it — his enemies turn their backs to him, and therefore it is called נפך (from עורף), as it is written of Yehudah: ״ידך בעורף אויביך״, and so we find strength of war and victory in the entire tribe of Yehudah: of David it is written (I Samuel 18:7): ״ודוד ברבבותיו״, and of the Messiah it is written (Isaiah 11:4): ״וברוח שפתיו ימות רשע״. <br><b>Yissakhar</b> upon sapir; it is what is called sappirin, and it is the color of tekhelet. It was given to Yissakhar because they were great in the wisdom of Torah, as it says (I Chronicles 12:33): ״ומבני יששכר יודעי בינה לעתים״ etc. And the tablets of the Torah were of sapirinon, and likewise we find at the giving of the Torah (Exodus 24:10): ״ותחת רגליו כמעשה לבנת הספיר״. And it is known that the soul of the masters of Torah is bound in the bond of life beneath the Throne of Glory, of which it is written (Ezekiel 1:26): ״כמראה אבן ספיר דמות כסא״. And this color of tekhelet is not a color of pride like the color of redness or the color of green that they call green (גרי״ן), both of which are colors of pride; rather, tekhelet is a color of humility and lowliness, fitting for young men and fitting for elders — and this is what is written (Isaiah 57:15): ״מרום וקדוש אשכון ואת דכא ושפל רוח״. The special property of this stone is that it is beneficial for the light of the eyes, and therefore one passes it over the eyes, and likewise the Torah illuminates the eyes; and they say this stone is beneficial for all pain and swelling wherever it may be in the body, and likewise the Torah heals the entire body, and so our Rabbis, of blessed memory, said: if one's head aches, let him engage in Torah. <br><b>Zevulun</b> upon yahalom; it is what is called pirla, and it is pure white, because silver is white, and it is a sign of the wealth of Zevulun, as it says (Genesis 49:13): ״זבולן לחוף ימים ישכון״ — therefore it was given to Zevulun, and it is beneficial for commerce. The special property of the stone is that it brings sleep upon a person, as Scripture says (Genesis 30:20): ״הפעם יזבלני אישי״. <br><b>Dan</b> upon leshem; it is what is called astefesis, and upon it is found a human face inverted, because the tribe of Dan inverted the bowl upon its mouth and turned there (לשם) into an idol in the pesel of Mikha; and it is written in the book of Joshua regarding the division of the land (Joshua 19:47): ״ויקראו ללשם דן כשם דן אביהם״ — for it was the name of a city in the land of Israel. <br><b>Naftali</b> upon shvo; it is called turkiza, and horsemen constantly carry it, and so its Aramaic translation is טרקיא. The special property of this stone is that it draws a person to a mount and makes him successful in the matter of riding all his days, and therefore it is called shvo; and because it joins him to the mount it was given to Naftali, which is language of joining, on account of (Genesis 30:8): ״נפתולי Elohim נפתלתי״. <br><b>Gad</b> upon ahlamah; it is what is called kristal, and therefore its Aramaic translation is עין עגלא, and the stone called liyam is similar to it except that it has some redness. The ahlamah was given to Gad because just as ahlamah, which is kristal, is commonly found among people and all recognize it, so too the tribe of Gad were numerous and their slain were conspicuous, as it is written (Deuteronomy 33:20): ״וטרף זרוע אף קדקד״. The special property of this stone is that it strengthens the heart so that it does not grow faint in going forth to war, and therefore it is called ahlamah, from the language of strength, as in what is said (Isaiah 38:16): ״ותחלימני והחייני״, and it is written (Job 39:4): ״יחלמו בניהם ירבו בר״. <br><b>Asher</b> upon tarshish; it is what is called kariolika, and its hue resembles the hue of oil. There are those who interpreted it as the stone whose color is tekhelet, and its Aramaic translation is כרום ימא, for the hue of the sea is like tekhelet; and we find a sea whose name is Tarshish, as it says (Jonah 1:3): ״וימצא אניה באה תרשיש״. The special property of this stone is to digest food, and all the more so for one who grinds it and mixes it into his food — he thereby becomes fat and oily, as it is written (Genesis 49:20): ״מאשר שמנה לחמו״. <br><b>Yosef</b> upon shoham; it is called onikli. The special property of this stone is to give favor to a person in the eyes of all who see him, and it is called shoham, which contains the letters of the Name, on account of (Genesis 39:21): ״ויהי YHWH את יוסף ויהי איש מצליח״, and (Genesis 39:4): ״ויתן חנו בעיני שר בית הסהר״ — and whoever carries it in the royal house, it will benefit him greatly and he will succeed and his words will be heard. <br><b>Binyamin</b> upon yashfeh; it is called yasfiza, and it is comprised of many hues — red, black, and green. The special property of this stone is to stop the flow of blood. It was given to Binyamin because his heart turned to many hues and he thought many thoughts regarding the sale of Yosef — whether to reveal the matter to his father or not — and nevertheless he conquered his inclination and held back his spirit and did not reveal the matter to his father, and therefore this stone was given to him. It is called yashfeh — two words: יש פה — and this alludes to his virtue, that even though he had a mouth and could have revealed the matter, he was silent and did not reveal it. These are the twelve stones set in the garment of the breastplate, and these are their names, and the names of the twelve tribes engraved upon them in the order of their birth, tribe by tribe written upon its particular stone — all of it in complete providence and wondrous intention." "<b>And I saw</b> in the Book of Stones composed by the philosopher, that stones require purity, and it is known from their special property that one who carries them in impurity — their power is nullified to him or is weakened, and if he repents and purifies himself the stone returns to its former strength as at first. The cause and the reason for this is according to the spiritual degrees of the supernal force that the stone draws from — which cleaves to purity and distances itself from impurity; and so the author wrote in his composition. And it is fitting to believe thus, for there is no precious stone that does not draw force from the supernal realms, in the manner that he said concerning herbs: there is no herb below that does not have a mazal above. From this you may learn that the High Priest in the garments of the ephod and the breastplate was surrounded from before and from behind by the names of the twelve tribes — the breastplate upon his heart from in front, upon which the names of the twelve tribes were set upon its twelve stones, each tribe upon its stone; and likewise the ephod, which was behind him upon his two shoulders, where the two shoham stones were set, upon which the twelve tribes were engraved — six upon this stone and six upon that stone. And the priest, when he wore them, wore them together — the ephod with the breastplate — for they were joined one to the other, as it says (Exodus 28:28): ״ולא יזח החשן מעל האפוד״. The breastplate was upon his heart from the front and the ephod from behind, and the heishnev of the ephod was tied upon his heart beneath the breastplate, and the two shoulder-pieces of the ephod upon his two shoulders, and the two chains of gold descending from above the shoulder-pieces on either side, set within the upper rings of the breastplate, and two threads of tekhelet fastened beneath his armpits from the two rings on the lower shoulder-pieces of the ephod that are above the heishnev." "<b>And by way of the Kabbalah</b> you will find wondrous and awe-inspiring wisdom in these twelve stones of the breastplate, in that each of the tribes was given one particular stone of a particular hue, all according to the hue of their attributes on high. Reuven, who alludes to the attribute of Judgment, his stone is odem; and Shim'on, who alludes to the six extremities, his stone is pitdah and its hue is green like keration; Levi, who alludes to the joining of the three above, his stone is bareket, whose light blazes and flashes; Yehudah, who alludes to Tohu, which is a green line, his stone is nophekh, which is green like keration; Zevulun, who alludes to the attribute of Mercy, his stone is yahalom, which is pirla, white; Yissakhar to sapir, whose hue is tekhelet tending toward tekhelet; Dan, who alludes to the attribute of Judgment like Reuven, his stone is leshem, and in it is the form of a man; Naftali, who alludes to Zachor, his stone is turkiza, which is shvo, and its species includes male and female; Yosef, who alludes to the attribute of Mercy like Zevulun, his stone is shoham, in which are the letters of the Name; Binyamin, who comprehends from all, his stone is yashfeh, which is comprised of many hues. And may the Place open our heart to gaze upon wonders from His Torah."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 28, :notes {3 "החבר refers to the author of the cited Book of Stones (most likely Aristotle's Lapidaire or a related lapidary). החשב read as החשב (heishnev, a technical term for the ephod's woven band; reading follows standard Torah commentary usage)."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:06:00.031229790Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}